<title>USA: Warm weather speeds Minnesota corn, soy growth.</title>
<headline>Warm weather speeds Minnesota corn, soy growth.</headline>
<dateline>ST PAUL, Minn. 1997-06-30</dateline>
<text>
<p>Warm, humid weather during the week ended Sunday boosted corn and soybean growth in Minnesota, with average corn height up nine inches during the week and average soybean height up three inches, the state's Agricultural Statistics Service said.</p>
<p>The average corn height was 23 inches versus the five-year average of 24 inches. The corn was rated 11 percent excellent, 46 good, 33 fair, eight poor and two very poor.</p>
<p>The average soybean height was eight inches, the same as the five-year average.</p>
<p>Spring wheat was 18 percent headed versus 29 a year ago and the 46 average. The crop was rated eight percent excellent, 44 good, 40 fair, six poor and two very poor.</p>
<p>Oats were rated six percent excellent, 40 good, 33 fair, 16 poor and five very poor.</p>
<p>Topsoil moisture was rated eight percent surplus, 50 adequate, 28 short and 14 very short.</p>
<p>((Chicago Newsdesk 312-408-8720))</p>
</text>
